<p>
Over at the <a href="https://www.technologyreview.com">MIT Technology Review</a> there’s a nice article on <a href="https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/10/20/1145399/emacs-turns-50/">Emacs turning 50 this year</a>. It gives a short history of Emacs starting from its TECO days. Most of that history is pretty well known to seasoned Emacs users but it’s still interesting.
</p>
<p>
What the article doesn’t give is the exact day of Emacs’ 50<sup>th</sup> birthday. That’s not surprising. The early history of Emacs involved the researchers at the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory agreeing on a standard set of TECO macros in order to improve collaboration. That was undoubtedly an ongoing process that didn’t have a well defined beginning or end so it’s easy to understand why the exact birthday of Emacs is a little murky.
</p>
<p>
Regardless, we can all celebrate Emacs’ 50<sup>th</sup> year. It’s remarkable when you think about it. Very few programs last that long. There’s FORTRAN and Lisp, of course, but I can’t think of any other piece of software with that sort of longevity. There is, to be sure, a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ship_of_Theseus">Ship of Theseus</a> aspect to that longevity but it’s one shared by FORTRAN and Lisp as well.
</p>
<p>
Who knows what things will look like in another 50 years but if we still have editors, I’m putting my money on Emacs still being around. Wouldn’t that be something?</p>
